    "body": "Reference check (per maintainer rule: maintainer-filed claims verified against public documentation; no other product's code or output).\n\nConfirms: Autodesk's published Setup docs describe the setup orientation as defining the WCS's own X/Y/Z axes, and the WCS as the coordinate system the post-processed toolpath is output in, matching the machine work offset (G54-G59) ([Fusion CAM Setup reference](https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/ENU/Fusion-CAM/files/MFG-REF-SETUP-MILL.htm), [WCS fundamentals](https://www.autodesk.com/products/fusion-360/blog/machining-fundamentals-introduction-work-coordinate-system-wcs/), [WCS vs tool orientation](https://www.autodesk.com/products/fusion-360/blog/fusion-360-wcs-tool-orientation/)). The expectation that orientation turns compose in the setup's frame - not a hidden model/world frame - is consistent with that documented model.\nContradicts: nothing found.\nUnclear: the docs do not spell out, for a generic \"turn about X+/Y+...\" button, which frame the rotation vector is read in; the WCS-relative reading is the documented behaviour of the setup result, but the exact control wording is not published. The G54-G59/G68 model does not settle the UI-frame question.\n",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
